1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,432
Summer, 1939.

2
00:00:04,132 --> 00:00:06,740
Across Europe, life goes on as normal.

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:12,053
But within weeks, the world would be plunged
into the deadliest war in human history.

4
00:00:12,553 --> 00:00:13,968
Countries would fall.

5
00:00:14,218 --> 00:00:15,487
Cities would burn.

6
00:00:15,737 --> 00:00:18,051
Tens of millions of people would die.

7
00:00:18,751 --> 00:00:20,611
So how did the world get here?

8
00:00:20,911 --> 00:00:22,635
And how did it finally end?

9
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,128
To understand the Second World War,
we have to start with the First.

10
00:00:35,448 --> 00:00:41,092
In November 1918, Germany's army was
beaten, and its emperor had abdicated.

11
00:00:41,412 --> 00:00:46,747
The following year, the Treaty of Versailles
made Germany accept responsibility for

12
00:00:46,747 --> 00:00:47,840
the war's damage.

13
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:53,854
It lost around 13 per cent of its European
territory and all of its colonies, its

14
00:00:53,854 --> 00:00:59,127
army was capped at 100,000 men, and it
was ordered to pay huge reparations.

15
00:00:59,447 --> 00:01:01,760
Many Germans felt humiliated.

16
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:06,733
Germany's new democracy, the Weimar
Republic, struggled from the start.

17
00:01:07,053 --> 00:01:11,640
Then, in 1929, the Wall Street Crash
triggered the Great Depression.

18
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:16,115
By 1932, around six million
Germans were out of work.

19
00:01:16,615 --> 00:01:19,590
But Versailles alone did
not cause another war.

20
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:25,749
Economic despair, fragile politics, nationalism
and antisemitism created an opening,

21
00:01:25,749 --> 00:01:28,530
and extremists were ready to exploit it.

22
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:35,221
One of those extremists was Adolf
Hitler, leader of the Nazi Party.

23
00:01:35,541 --> 00:01:40,722
Nazi propaganda offered simple answers to
complicated problems, blaming Germany's

24
00:01:40,722 --> 00:01:44,496
troubles on Jewish people,
communists and the peace treaty.

25
00:01:44,816 --> 00:01:48,768
In January 1933, Hitler
was appointed Chancellor.

26
00:01:49,088 --> 00:01:53,594
Within months, he had banned rival
parties, crushed the free press and turned

27
00:01:53,594 --> 00:01:55,233
Germany into a dictatorship.

28
00:01:55,553 --> 00:01:59,812
Nazi ideology was racist,
antisemitic and expansionist.

29
00:02:00,132 --> 00:02:04,819
Jewish Germans were stripped of their rights,
while Hitler rebuilt the armed forces.

30
00:02:05,219 --> 00:02:09,080
In 1936, German troops
marched into the Rhineland.

31
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:12,861
In March 1938, Germany annexed Austria.

32
00:02:13,181 --> 00:02:18,340
That September, at Munich, Britain and
France let Hitler take the Sudetenland from

33
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:20,919
Czechoslovakia, hoping to keep the peace.

34
00:02:21,239 --> 00:02:22,187
It didn't work.

35
00:02:22,487 --> 00:02:28,575
In March 1939, Germany occupied the rest
of the Czech lands. Appeasement had failed.

36
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:35,341
Hitler's next target was Poland.
But first, he made a shocking deal.

37
00:02:35,661 --> 00:02:41,535
In August 1939, Nazi Germany and the
Soviet Union, bitter enemies, signed a

38
00:02:41,535 --> 00:02:43,101
non-aggression pact.

39
00:02:43,421 --> 00:02:47,278
A secret protocol divided
Eastern Europe between them.

40
00:02:47,778 --> 00:02:53,160
On 1 September 1939, German
forces invaded Poland.

41
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:58,851
Tanks and aircraft struck with terrifying
speed, though the Polish army fought back hard.

42
00:02:59,171 --> 00:03:03,066
Two days later, Britain and
France declared war on Germany.

43
00:03:03,386 --> 00:03:06,169
The Second World War in Europe had begun.

44
00:03:06,569 --> 00:03:12,168
Then, on 17 September, the Soviet
Union invaded Poland from the east.

45
00:03:12,488 --> 00:03:14,945
Within weeks, Poland had been carved up.

46
00:03:15,345 --> 00:03:22,514
But war had already come to Asia. Japan
had invaded Manchuria in 1931, and launched

47
00:03:22,514 --> 00:03:25,883
a full-scale war against China in 1937.

48
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:34,081
For months, the Western
Front was eerily quiet.

49
00:03:34,401 --> 00:03:39,323
Then, in April 1940, Germany
invaded Denmark and Norway.

50
00:03:39,643 --> 00:03:44,027
In May, it struck the Netherlands,
Belgium, Luxembourg and France.

51
00:03:44,347 --> 00:03:50,207
German tanks pushed through the Ardennes forest
and raced for the coast, trapping Allied armies.

52
00:03:50,527 --> 00:03:56,754
From Dunkirk, more than 330,000 troops were
evacuated across the Channel, helped by

53
00:03:56,754 --> 00:03:58,780
a flotilla of little ships.

54
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:01,054
But in June, France fell.

55
00:04:01,454 --> 00:04:06,249
Britain's new Prime Minister, Winston
Churchill, refused to make peace.

56
00:04:06,569 --> 00:04:11,596
That summer, in the Battle of Britain, the
Royal Air Force fought off the Luftwaffe.

57
00:04:11,916 --> 00:04:18,403
So Germany bombed British cities instead. More
than 40,000 civilians were killed in the Blitz.

58
00:04:18,723 --> 00:04:20,405
But Britain kept fighting.

59
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:38,946
On 22 June 1941, Hitler launched Operation
Barbarossa, the invasion of the Soviet Union.

60
00:04:39,266 --> 00:04:45,555
More than three million Axis soldiers poured
across a front nearly 3,000 kilometres long.

61
00:04:45,875 --> 00:04:51,249
The Eastern Front would become the largest
and deadliest theatre of the entire war.

62
00:04:51,569 --> 00:04:57,692
German forces reached the outskirts of Moscow,
but as winter set in, the Red Army struck back.

63
00:04:58,092 --> 00:05:02,169
Meanwhile, Japan was expanding
across Asia and the Pacific.

64
00:05:02,489 --> 00:05:09,414
On 7 December 1941, Japanese aircraft
attacked the American naval base at Pearl

65
00:05:09,414 --> 00:05:10,992
Harbor, in Hawaii.

66
00:05:11,312 --> 00:05:14,757
More than 2,400 Americans were killed.

67
00:05:15,077 --> 00:05:21,983
The United States declared war on Japan. Days
later, Germany declared war on the United States.

68
00:05:22,303 --> 00:05:27,701
Now the war was truly global, fought across
Europe, Africa, Asia and the Pacific.

69
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:34,147
In 1942, the tide began to turn.

70
00:05:34,466 --> 00:05:40,077
In June, at the Battle of Midway, American
forces sank four Japanese aircraft

71
00:05:40,077 --> 00:05:43,793
carriers, a blow Japan's
navy never recovered from.

72
00:05:44,113 --> 00:05:49,894
In North Africa, British and Commonwealth
forces under General Montgomery defeated

73
00:05:49,894 --> 00:05:51,515
the Axis at El Alamein.

74
00:05:51,835 --> 00:05:57,320
And at Stalingrad, on the River Volga, the
Red Army encircled more than a quarter of

75
00:05:57,320 --> 00:05:58,887
a million Axis soldiers.

76
00:05:59,207 --> 00:06:05,831
In February 1943, the survivors surrendered.
It was a catastrophic defeat for Germany.

77
00:06:06,231 --> 00:06:08,594
No single battle won the war.

78
00:06:08,914 --> 00:06:12,308
But the Allies were also
winning a battle of production.

79
00:06:12,628 --> 00:06:18,115
American, Soviet and British factories
turned out tanks, aircraft and ships faster

80
00:06:18,115 --> 00:06:20,189
than the Axis could ever match.

81
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:35,826
By 1944, the Allies were ready
to open a new front in the west.

82
00:06:36,146 --> 00:06:39,569
On 6 June, Operation Overlord began.

83
00:06:39,889 --> 00:06:46,314
In the largest seaborne invasion in
history, around 156,000 American, British,

84
00:06:46,314 --> 00:06:51,915
Canadian and other Allied troops
landed on five beaches in Normandy.

85
00:06:52,235 --> 00:06:57,312
Thousands were killed or wounded on the
first day alone, but by nightfall, the

86
00:06:57,312 --> 00:06:59,395
Allies had a foothold in France.

87
00:06:59,715 --> 00:07:05,869
As they fought their way inland, resistance
fighters sabotaged railways and supply lines.

88
00:07:06,189 --> 00:07:09,806
In August 1944, Paris was liberated.

89
00:07:10,206 --> 00:07:15,441
Meanwhile, in the east, a huge Soviet
offensive smashed through German lines.

90
00:07:15,761 --> 00:07:21,143
Germany was now fighting on several fronts,
and running out of men, fuel and time.

91
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:36,122
In 1945, Allied armies closed
in on Germany from both sides.

92
00:07:36,442 --> 00:07:40,598
As they advanced, they uncovered
the full horror of Nazi rule.

93
00:07:41,098 --> 00:07:46,339
In the concentration and death camps,
soldiers found starving survivors and the

94
00:07:46,339 --> 00:07:47,931
evidence of mass murder.

95
00:07:48,431 --> 00:07:54,163
In the Holocaust, Nazi Germany and its
collaborators systematically murdered around

96
00:07:54,163 --> 00:07:57,132
six million Jewish men,
women and children.

97
00:07:57,732 --> 00:08:03,602
Millions of others were also persecuted and
killed, including Roma, disabled people,

98
00:08:03,602 --> 00:08:06,747
Polish civilians and
Soviet prisoners of war.

99
00:08:07,447 --> 00:08:10,819
In April, Soviet troops
fought their way into Berlin.

100
00:08:11,139 --> 00:08:16,789
On 30 April 1945, Hitler took
his own life in his bunker.

101
00:08:17,109 --> 00:08:23,071
Days later, Germany surrendered
unconditionally, and on 8 May, crowds celebrated

102
00:08:23,071 --> 00:08:24,710
Victory in Europe Day.

103
00:08:25,110 --> 00:08:27,455
But in the Pacific, the war went on.

104
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:36,779
By 1945, American forces were closing
in on Japan, island by island.

105
00:08:37,099 --> 00:08:44,050
The Battle of Okinawa, from April to June,
killed well over 100,000 soldiers and civilians.

106
00:08:44,370 --> 00:08:47,474
Japan's leaders still
refused to surrender.

107
00:08:47,974 --> 00:08:53,076
On 6 August, the United States
dropped an atomic bomb on Hiroshima.

108
00:08:53,396 --> 00:08:58,560
Tens of thousands of people died instantly,
and many more in the months that followed.

109
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:05,043
On 8 August, the Soviet Union declared
war on Japan and invaded Manchuria.

110
00:09:05,363 --> 00:09:09,373
The next day, a second atomic
bomb devastated Nagasaki.

111
00:09:09,773 --> 00:09:16,756
On 15 August, Emperor Hirohito announced
Japan's surrender, which was formally

112
00:09:16,756 --> 00:09:19,173
signed on 2 September 1945.

113
00:09:19,493 --> 00:09:25,738
Historians still debate how much the bombs and
the Soviet invasion each shaped that decision.

114
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:36,509
The war killed an estimated 70 to 85
million people, most of them civilians.

115
00:09:36,829 --> 00:09:41,879
From the ruins came the United Nations,
and a new rivalry: the Cold War.

116
00:09:42,279 --> 00:09:47,685
The war showed how quickly hatred and
unchecked aggression can lead to catastrophe.

117
00:09:48,005 --> 00:09:51,094
But it also showed the
courage of ordinary people.

118
00:09:51,494 --> 00:09:55,014
Because history isn't just
about battles or leaders.

119
00:09:55,214 --> 00:09:58,219
It's about people. And
every person has a story.

